WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.260
Good morning Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.580
Today I'm going to endeavor to explain

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.100
what the heck "sequestration" is,

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.020
why it's so important, and

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.000
why it's so bananas, but first

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.400
we have to understand a few things about

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.420
what governments do.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.700
So, there are a lot of government programs that

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.140
are super popular with people,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.460
Like, for instance, here in the U.S.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:14.640
we have something called "Medicare", which

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.080
offers inexpensive health care to older people.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.860
Now, Medicare's actually more efficient and cheaper

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.480
per person than private insurance,

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.300
but it is still very expensive,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.260
because, don't take this personally old people,

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.100
but you get sick a lot.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.420
We also have a very popular program that

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.180
offers health care to our poor citizens

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:28.740
called "Medicaid"

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.500
and between those two, that's 23%

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.520
of the federal budget.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:32.940
Another 22% of the budget

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.020
goes to Social Security,

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.300
a very popular program in which

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.800
old people get checks every month

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.080
so they can buy, you know, like, food

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:38.780
and computers.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:39.680
By the way, I know that they're

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.760
buying computers, because

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.000
suddenly a lot of old people are reading

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.140
"The Fault in Our Stars", and

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.280
they keep e-mailing me

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.700
and the subject line is, like,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.820
in all capital letters,

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.420
"MY GRANDDAUGHTER RECOMMENDED THAT I

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:49.680
READ YOUR BOOK BECAUSE SHE

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.980
THINKS THAT I AM ABOUT TO DIE

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.080
I THOUGHT IT WAS FUNNY AND

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.400
SAD, GOOD JOB"

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.280
and then there's nothing in the

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:54.960
body of the e-mail.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.340
So, we need Social Security

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.620
because how else am I gonna hear

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.000
from my older readers?

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.360
Right, so those three programs

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.060
are 45% of the federal budget.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.600
and they're what's known as "mandatory spending programs".

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.600
There's an additional 13% of our budget

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.100
that is also mandatory spending,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.080
like salaries for federal judges and stuff,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.660
and then the interest on our national debt

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:12.940
is 6% of our budget,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.600
So, in total, that stuff is 64% of the federal budget.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.960
The other 36% is "discretionary spending",

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.760
which congress allocates yearly,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.320
or sometimes over multiple years,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.380
or sometimes only in 90-day segments

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.340
if they're feeling very cranky and inefficient.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.900
That's everything from, like, veterans' affairs

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.700
to building highways, to NASA, to

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.940
science research, to meat inspectors...

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.200
But more than half of all discretionary spending

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.580
doesn't go to any of that stuff, because

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.740
it goes to defense.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.180
One other piece of background information:

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.880
as previously noted, the United States has a long-term

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:41.820
debt problem, but

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.160
it does not have a short-term debt problem.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.080
Like, right now the U.S. can borrow money extremely cheaply,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.700
arguably, like, cheaper than free.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.380
And that's because we're a very strong economy,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.800
we've never defaulted on our debt,

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.100
there's no reason to think that we will...

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.080
and our budget deficit, the annual difference between

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.280
what we take in and what we spend,

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.680
is shrinking pretty fast

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.280
and is probably not a concern at the moment.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.760
But there is a serious long-term problem with our

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.160
mandatory spending programs.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.660
The cost of Medicare and Medicaid

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.080
will double over the next 30 years,

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.580
as the population ages and health care costs continue to rise

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.480
and the cost of Social Security will go up too,

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.960
so in the long term we either have to decrease benefits

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.780
or increase revenues or both,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.580
or else our budget deficit will spiral,

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.040
and our debt will get more expensive,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:18.860
and Greece.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.500
So we have a long-term problem,

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.860
which requires a long-term solution,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.140
and that brings is to the Sequester.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.600
Right, so back in 2011,

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.100
the U.S. nearly defaulted on its sovereign debt,

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.040
not because we couldn't afford to pay the debt,

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.860
but because congress was refusing to raise the debt ceiling,

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.340
which is particularly hilarious because

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.460
the reason we were reaching the debt ceiling

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.880
is because of budgets that congress had passed.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.880
There seems to be a little bit of confusion about this,

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.420
but one, in the United States congress sets the tax rates,

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.440
and two, in the United States congress sets the budget.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.360
And so if the budget and the tax rates lead to a deficit,

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.860
that is congress' fault!

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:52.720
Literally!

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.140
Presidents may, like, drop by congress to try to argue for whatever they believe in,

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.200
but presidents do not set tax rates

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.960
and they do not decide the budget.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.400
So eventually congress passed the "Budget Control Act"

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.480
in which they raised the debt ceiling in order to

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.440
pay for the budget that they themselves had created.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.480
And in this Budget Control Act, congress created

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.560
a so-called "super-committee"

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.940
that would address the nation's long-term fiscal problems

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.800
with a "grand bargain" that would

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.440
keep our debt from spiraling out of control and Greece-ing.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.680
And in order to force the super-committee to do its job,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.020
congress enacted another ticking time bomb,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.680
because we don't have enough of those,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.580
that was originally proposed by President Obama

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.860
called "Sequestration".

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.100
If they didn't come to an agreement, there would be automatic cuts

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.660
of 1.2 trillion dollars over ten years,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:35.040
half cuts to defense and half cuts to other spending,

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.140
but vitally, very little cuts to the mandatory spending programs

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.700
that are the center of the long-term fiscal problem!

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.100
Which is such an incredibly bad idea that the assumption was

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.120
they would find a way to compromise, but, yeah, no.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.620
Think if a doctor told you you had a slow-growing tumor in your fingernail

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.160
and you responded by saying "Quick, remove my gall bladder!"

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.740
Right, so the reason that sequestration is so bananas

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:55.280
is not because of the cuts themselves,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.980
it's because they're indiscriminate.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.360
Every project, program and activity of the United States federal government

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.920
has to be cut by the exact same percentages

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.580
except for those mandatory spending programs

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.360
that are mostly exempt.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.780
So, basically, everything the government currently does,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.920
it has to do about 8% less of.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.200
That doesn't sound that bad,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.040
but because it's every project, program, and activity

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.640
it gets super bananas.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.520
For instance, something sort of like this happened in 1991,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.020
with a 5% sequestration,

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:23.400
and federal employees were literally instructed

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.160
to scrape 5% less bird poop off buoys in Chesapeake Bay,

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:32.020
because that is a project, program, or activity of the federal government.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.480
So, instead of making a choice about

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.640
which federal programs we can afford to get rid of,

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.700
we're just going to scrape 8% less poop off of all the buoys.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:39.740
That is bananas.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.940
It's gonna cost between half a million and two million jobs,

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.080
and instead of getting rid of jobs because they're outdated or unnecessary,

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.340
it basically just throws a dart at a dartboard.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.260
And more importantly, sequestration doesn't just create problems,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.260
it also fails to solve problems,

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.660
because it does absolutely nothing to address

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.680
the long-term fiscal problem in the U.S,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.680
because mandatory spending on Medicare, Medicaid, and Social Security

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.400
is gonna continue to grow at pretty much the exact same pace.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.840
So, essentially, Hank, congress saw a long-term problem

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.060
which they responded to by creating a short-term crisis,

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.620
which they then failed to solve.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.680
These days the conversation on the news channels

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.920
tends to be about whose terrible idea this was

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.620
with congressional republicans being like

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.060
"This was President Obama's idea",

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:18.240
and President Obama being like

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.200
"I'm sorry, do I create and pass legislation in this country?"

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.400
But Hank, this whole business about

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:23.360
only discussing who's to blame

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.160
distracts from the larger issue, which is

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.520
that the sequestration is a disaster!

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.300
It's inefficient and bureaucratic and everything that people don't like about government

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.660
and it does nothing to make our economy healthier.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.620
So, to summarize, the sequester creates an unnecessary catastrophe

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.640
without addressing the larger future catastrophe.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.080
It is an embarrassment.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.940
Frankly, Hank, it is more embarrassing than our

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.420
continued minting of pennies, and

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:45.640
that's saying something!

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:46.560
I'll see you on Friday.

